<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Craig Roberts - Full Pack Part 3</title></head><body><h1>Craig Roberts - Full Pack Part 3</h1><p>Craig Roberts — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 3)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>04-01 Performance Review</p><p>Plaud file id: ff2a87a16cf698d40a043afb96e50cda</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance and Strategy Review for Craig Roberts</p><p>Bob Duncan led a monthly performance review with advisor Craig Roberts, focusing on the previous month’s results and the practical drivers behind them. While the month landed just under £13k and mortgage activity was broadly in line with peers, the discussion repeatedly returned to two income levers that materially held the result back: **average protection premium** and **the percentage of cases charged an admin fee**. From there, they explored how Craig’s pipeline is developing (with many clients still viewing and not yet proceeding), how upcoming **Platform** workflows could automate nurturing and task management, and a strategic proposal to introduce a second advisor (Karen) to increase lead generation—explicitly framed as **uplift**, not redistribution.</p><p>---</p><p>## Monthly Performance and Financial Metrics Review</p><p>Bob confirmed Craig’s month at **£12,978** (described as “just under thirteen”). He noted there was **one report still to go out for David Gillen**, but overall the expectation was that there would not be much additional revenue beyond what was already counted.</p><p>They reviewed two notable pieces of lost/changed business:</p><p>- A **large “Not Taken Up” (NTO)** case “came off”. Craig explained it ultimately went to a **different lender**.</p><p>- A **remortgage** also came off. Craig described initially keying it with **Barclays**, but the sourced rate was not showing. He then chose **Skipton**, which was “**two pence more expensive**”, and submitted the application. He acknowledged he did not go back to confirm whether the issue was his error or whether the rate had been pulled that day—partly because he did not want to create doubt by telling the client lenders were pulling rates on the day if it turned out to be his mistake.</p><p>Craig’s operational feel for the month was that he was “busy-ish” in a positive way: appointment numbers were “lowish” in volume, but **high in quality**, with recent appointments involving people who were actively buying; the main friction was **obtaining documents** and progressing them through to closing.</p><p>---</p><p>## Analysis of Performance Gaps and Improvement Areas</p><p>Bob’s diagnosis was that Craig’s headline gap was **not mortgage volume**, but the income attached to cases—especially protection value and fees.</p><p>Key comparative metrics and gaps discussed:</p><p>- **Protection penetration**: Craig’s penetration was described as **the best so far for the quarter**, and Bob noted Craig was **one more protection case** away from being “bang on **50%**”.</p><p>- **Average protection premium**: This was the central concern. Craig’s average protection cases were **twelve hundred**, while the group average was **almost two grands**—identified as the primary reason performance was being “pulled down”.</p><p>- **Proc fee**: Craig’s proc fee was **5 hundred**, described as slightly better than the group.</p><p>- **Average case size**: Craig’s case size was described as “okay”, at **sixteen hundred** (group: just under sixteen hundred).</p><p>- **Admin fee charging rate**: This compounded the issue. Across the group, about **55 percent** of clients were being charged a fee; Craig was at **38 percent**.</p><p>Craig provided context for the protection premium weakness: several “great big mortgages” were attached to circumstances where clients were disinclined to take protection, often due to the **short-term nature of the lending**. He cited examples including a client intending to repay in **a few months to a year**, and a larger **buy-to-let** plan expected to be cleared in **twelve months plus** via sale of another property—leading those clients to decline protection consideration.</p><p>At quarter and year-end level, Bob framed protection as the consistent missing component:</p><p>- Year-end position referenced: **almost eighty cases**, with **thirty cases with insurance** (**thirty eight percent**), leaving Craig **ten cases adrift**.</p><p>- Bob also highlighted a forward-looking performance control: Craig’s projected income needed to be **30 k, plus 35 k** to keep him reliably within bonus territory quarter-on-quarter.</p><p>- Pipeline shape was also noted: Craig’s pipeline was “building for **next quarter** rather than this quarter”, contributing to being “a wee bit behind” for the quarter—again attributed mainly to **insurance**, and secondarily to **fees**.</p><p>---</p><p>## Operational Efficiency and Pipeline Management</p><p>Craig described a workload dominated by clients who are actively viewing properties but not yet securing one. He receives weekly requests from such clients for payment reminders to support offers, but many are still “losing out and still viewing”. His process includes:</p><p>- If he does not hear from a client for about **a month**, he passes them to **Sean**.</p><p>- He gave an example of a client (a footballer and partner) who have been offering and viewing since just after Christmas; he speaks to her “most days”.</p><p>Bob validated improvement in Craig’s day-to-day control:</p><p>- Craig had only **two overdue tasks**, with one **seven days** old.</p><p>- The seven-day overdue item related to an appointment for **Lewis McCourt** (also referenced as being from **June 25th**).</p><p>- Both agreed workflow management was **better than it was before**, and Bob stressed that maintaining a live pipeline should reduce the impact of future quiet periods.</p><p>### Platform rollout and workflow automation</p><p>They discussed the **Platform** system and how it will reshape tasking, nurturing, and document handling:</p><p>- Rollout of mortgage sourcing and suitability within the platform had been **delayed**, but was expected to go live **this month**, with training to follow.</p><p>- Craig had already encountered live usage signals: clients uploading documents, receiving emails when clients accept terms of business, and occasional confusion (e.g., a client reporting the system was “locked”).</p><p>- A specific example: **Mark Beverstock** uploaded documents including bank statements; Craig also mentioned an ID-check mismatch where Mark’s appearance did not fully match a **2021** passport photo.</p><p>- Bob described a platform capability where the system queried whether a **passport** could be used as proof of income, and the “AI boss” determined it could.</p><p>Bob walked through how platform workflows should function operationally:</p><p>- Workflows can flip tasks between roles (e.g., advisor and support).</p><p>- If an advisor does not update a task, the lead manager (Sean for Craig) prompts for an update.</p><p>- Outcomes of appointments are categorised (e.g., business, waste of time, debt/app required, early looking **3–6 months out**, not convenient, actively hunting).</p><p>- Active property hunters can be diarised for contact and moved into nurture journeys.</p><p>- The nurture journey can involve Megan making a call, then returning the case to the advisor if the client remains active.</p><p>- A workflow error was observed in demonstration (after offer accepted and appointment booked, it bounced to the wrong place), but the intended end-to-end path ultimately supports progress through to submitting an application, with mortgage writing transferred into **Midas** for now (as sourcing/writing cannot currently be done directly in Platform).</p><p>They also discussed an operational change in who handles what:</p><p>- The process is being adjusted so that only true admin tasks (and small actions like placing clients into nurture journeys) go to **Anna**, while nurture calls remain with **Sean** or the advisor—intended to use everyone’s time more appropriately.</p><p>---</p><p>## Strategic Proposal: Team Restructuring and Lead Generation</p><p>Bob proposed a “double prong” approach: introducing **Karen** as a second advisor voice in the branch to increase total opportunities and diary capacity. He grounded the discussion in current lead distribution:</p><p>- Karen received **seventeen** leads for the month versus Craig’s **forty four** from Bearsden.</p><p>Core elements of the proposal:</p><p>- Karen would act as a “second voice” to support the journey, ideally covering times Craig is not in, and providing **weekend cover** (aligning with a wish from Maitland and Lewis, and with Karen’s personal preference for longer weekends to visit her mum).</p><p>- Bob suggested Karen’s more vocal style could complement Craig’s calm, positive style, potentially improving how lead quality issues are fed back and addressed.</p><p>- The proposal was repeatedly positioned as **not to Craig’s detriment**, but to generate a **net uplift** in leads and opportunities.</p><p>Craig expressed natural reservations (advisors do not want to miss opportunities), and also flagged practical constraints:</p><p>- He would struggle to commit to working **every second weekend** due to football.</p><p>- Weekend appointments were, at present, often being booked into the week rather than the weekend itself.</p><p>Success conditions were explicitly stated:</p><p>- The aim is **more leads overall**, not redistribution.</p><p>- If opportunities remain the same after **three months**, the trial has not worked (no uplift).</p><p>- If weekend appointments merely shift timing (weekend instead of weekday) and numbers stay flat, the system is not working; in that case, Karen’s working week might need reassessment.</p><p>- Bob also outlined a preferred client-facing approach: offer weekday options, then present Saturday as an availability option (especially if Saturday availability is limited), rather than announcing Saturday openings without ensuring the broader uplift objective is met.</p><p>---</p><p>## Future Targets and Tactical Adjustments</p><p>Bob reiterated the scale of the target gap: Craig had wanted to deliver **forty five** by a certain point, leaving **fifteen K** outstanding, and Bob referenced **twenty five k** as “a big number to deliver”. Craig felt there was enough business potential but needed “a bit of fair wind”.</p><p>The tactical focus was straightforward: protect the mortgage opportunity while lifting protection and fees.</p><p>- Craig described adjusting his approach by raising protection earlier—asking what cover clients already have before they “sneak away out the site”, rather than leaving protection until after mortgage momentum is established.</p><p>- He shared an example of prompting a client for existing policies; the client replied they had nothing and needed life insurance, leading Craig to plan a quote and call “tomorrow”.</p><p>Bob’s longer-range framing was to maintain trajectory, “tick off the insurance”, and then begin thinking about next year’s target—referencing an expected finish around **110, 120** and positioning next year’s target-setting as a forthcoming discussion.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>### **@Craig Roberts**</p><p>- [ ] Play with the new Platform system and its features — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Approve documents uploaded by clients on the Platform so they automatically transfer into Midas — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Keep an eye on overdue tasks, specifically Lewis McCourt’s appointment (referenced as from June 25th / seven days overdue) — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] When active clients are searching for properties, change their status in the Platform to **“nurture”** and **“searching for properties”** so they receive automated emails — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Send clients who go quiet to Sean for nudging — [TBD]</p><p>### **@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Speak to Alan about having a chat (and likely arranging a meeting) with Maitland and Lewis to run a trial of the proposed working arrangement — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Confirm Maitland and Lewis are still up for the trial — [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Go back to Karen and explain that the new working arrangement will be trialled for a period of time — [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Craig Roberts  </p><p>**Creation date:** 2026-04-01 11:30:53  </p><p>**Review date:** 2026-04-01  </p><p>**Reviewer/Manager:** Bob Duncan  </p><p>---</p><p>## Leads Performance Review (with CRM Discipline)</p><p>### A. Leads — Last Month</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **44 leads** recorded for the month.</p><p>- Pipeline outcomes referenced:</p><p>  - **“Good number of DIPs completed”** (exact count not stated).</p><p>  - **4** leads still showing as **“appointment booked”** (status hygiene check required).</p><p>  - **No-shows:** Craig reports **~1 per week**, including **1 no-show on 2026-04-01**.</p><p>- **9 leads marked “created Sean”** (ownership/source accuracy needs audit).</p><p>- **CRM notes volume referenced by Craig:** **“four or five notes… probably are mine”** (low note evidence relative to lead volume and weekly activity described).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior-week/prior-month comparison metrics provided (other than last-month totals above).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- **Lead volume is strong (44)** and described as steady/strong.</p><p>- Craig reports **appointment quality is higher**, with **last two buyers having bought** and moving into **document collection**.</p><p>- Workflow usage observed as improved in the period discussed (only **2 overdue tasks** noted elsewhere in the review set).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Ownership/reporting integrity gap:** **9 “created Sean”** leads create uncertainty (referral vs duplicate vs mis-assigned ownership).</p><p>- **Stale statuses:** **4 “appointment booked”** still showing as booked suggests outcomes are not being recorded promptly.</p><p>- **Follow-up leakage:** **~1 no-show per week** reducing conversion efficiency.</p><p>- **CRM discipline risk:** only **4–5 notes** referenced by Craig suggests **interactions/next steps may not be consistently captured**.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Reporting risk:** mis-stated performance if “created Sean” leads and appointment outcomes are not cleaned up.</p><p>- **Forecasting risk:** stale appointment outcomes inflate pipeline health and hide drift.</p><p>- **Conversion risk:** repeated no-shows without structured rebooking/chase tasks.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>1. **Audit the 9 “created Sean” leads** (confirm referral/duplication/ownership; correct statuses and owner).</p><p>2. **Review the 4 leads showing “appointment booked”** and update outcomes (attended / no-show / DIP / application / closed-lost).</p><p>3. Increase **CRM note capture** so each meaningful interaction has a note and a clear next step recorded.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- Audit “created Sean” leads: **Other (Sean) + Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>- Appointment status cleanup + note/next step discipline: **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant (data integrity risk):** lead volume is strong, but **ownership ambiguity and stale statuses** limit confidence in reporting and pipeline accuracy.</p><p>---</p><p>### C. Leads — Pipeline Follow-up Discipline (Task Hygiene)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **2 overdue workflow items** identified.</p><p>- One overdue is **7 days old**: **Lewis McCourt** (Craig believes it relates to an appointment; next referenced appointment date is **25 June** (year not stated)).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior overdue/task metrics provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Craig states he is **checking workflow daily**.</p><p>- Clear (informal) approach described: if no contact/updates after **~1 month**, cases are **passed to Sean**.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Overdue task aged 7 days (Lewis McCourt)** indicates follow-up slippage.</p><p>- **Handover to Sean** is described operationally but may not be consistently **logged with an explicit CRM next step/ownership change**.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Missed follow-up risk:** overdue tasks reduce conversion chance and increase disengagement.</p><p>- **Ownership gaps:** informal handovers risk creating **orphaned cases** without clear responsibility.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>1. **Lewis McCourt:** confirm status, update CRM, and either progress or formally hand over with a logged next step.</p><p>2. Keep overdue workflow items at **0–1 maximum** with same-day triage.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving but fragile:** overall workflow discipline is better, but a **7-day overdue** shows drift can still occur.</p><p>---</p><p>## “Where Are My Leads?” (Diary/Pipeline Allocation Risk)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Craig reports **“two or three appointments a weekend booked in”** by the branch team (booked into diary; not necessarily weekend appointments).</p><p>- A proposed change adds a second adviser “voice” (**Karen**) into weekend/branch cover.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No appointment booking trend data provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Weekend booking activity is supporting **week-start diary momentum**.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Risk that Karen’s involvement **redistributes existing opportunities** rather than creating uplift.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Lead/diary dilution risk:** if total opportunities remain the same post-trial, the model has not worked.</p><p>- **Availability constraint:** Craig stated weekend cover is difficult due to commitments, limiting flexibility if weekend appointments are expected.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>1. Agree and document a **trial plan** for Karen’s weekend/branch support focused on measurable **uplift** (not redistribution).</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Other (Bob / Branch leadership)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** activity exists, but allocation changes could reduce Craig’s effective opportunity flow without clear success measures.</p><p>---</p><p>## Applications in Progress</p><p>### Mark Beverstock — Lender: Atom Bank (sourcing list also shows Kent Alliance)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **Offer accepted** (confirmed).</p><p>- Client has uploaded multiple documents to **Platform** (bank statements and other requested items).</p><p>- Process confirmed: once documents are **approved in Platform**, they **auto-transfer into Midas**.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior stage comparison provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Strong **client engagement** with Platform document upload.</p><p>- Craig is now able to locate and use uploaded documents due to first-hand Platform use.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Document location/process inconsistency:** Craig initially checked the **financial services mailbox** rather than Platform.</p><p>- **ID check issue:** returned **“not quite matching”** (exact mismatch not specified).</p><p>- **Sourcing confusion risk:** Atom Bank/Kent Alliance both visible; concern client has “picked some obscure lenders” in sourcing list.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Case delay if documents are not **approved** in Platform (won’t flow into Midas).</p><p>- **ID verification discrepancy** could block progression.</p><p>- Lender/sourcing ambiguity could create processing errors and delays.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>1. **Approve uploaded Platform documents** so they transfer into Midas.</p><p>2. **Investigate and resolve** the **ID check “not quite matching”** issue.</p><p>3. Confirm lender direction and ensure sourcing/lender selection is **adviser-controlled and documented**.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving:** Platform engagement is helping document collection; progression depends on timely approvals and resolving ID mismatch.</p><p>---</p><p>## Protection Activity / Protection in Progress</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Protection remains the **primary performance gap**.</p><p>- Year-to-date referenced:</p><p>  - **~80 cases** YTD.</p><p>  - **30 cases with insurance** = **38% penetration**.</p><p>  - Craig stated as **~10 cases adrift** vs expectation.</p><p>- Value metrics:</p><p>  - **Average protection premium:** **£1,200** vs group **~£2,000** (main drag).</p><p>  - **Proc fee:** Craig **~£500** (broadly in line/slightly better than group referenced).</p><p>  - **Average case size:** Craig **~£1,600** vs group **just under £1,600** (in line).</p><p>  - **Fee charging rate:** Craig **38%** vs group **55%**.</p><p>- Behavioural change in progress:</p><p>  - Craig is raising protection earlier; one client confirmed **no existing protection** and Craig planned a **life insurance quote call on 2026-04-02** (client name not captured).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior period penetration/premium figures provided (qualitative comment that penetration is Craig’s “best so far for the quarter”).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Penetration has improved within the quarter (qualitative).</p><p>- Craig is consciously improving scripting (recognised he did not say “protection” enough).</p><p>- A protection next step has been diarised for at least one client (**life insurance quote call: 2026-04-02**).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Average premium is materially low** vs group, reducing protection revenue even when penetration improves.</p><p>- Craig may be pre-judging some clients as unlikely to proceed, reducing recommendation frequency/value.</p><p>- **Fee charging consistency** below group norm.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Protection remains the biggest risk to quarter/year income delivery.</p><p>- If protection is not consistently diarised and outcome-logged, it remains dependent on client timing rather than process.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>1. For every client progressing to DIP/application, **book a protection next step at the same time** and record the outcome in CRM (has cover / no cover / quote booked / declined with reason).</p><p>2. Focus on improving **average premium per protection case** through consistent, confident recommendation (no numeric target agreed).</p><p>3. Improve **fee-charging consistency** and record the fee conversation outcome in CRM.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Mixed:** **Improving (penetration/process intent)** but **declining (value)** due to low average premium.</p><p>---</p><p>## Written Business — This Month</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Written business shown as **12.978** for the month.</p><p>- **1 report outstanding:** **David Gillen** (not yet issued).</p><p>- Notable reporting movement:</p><p>  - A **large NTO** came off and back on due to **switching lender**.</p><p>  - A **remortgage** keyed with **Barclays** switched to **Skipton** due to a rate issue (“two pence more expensive”).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior month benchmark stated.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Month described as **“not that bad.”**</p><p>- Workload more manageable when deals are in **offer/closing** stage.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Month-end admin slippage:** **David Gillen** report not issued at month-end.</p><p>- **Process control risk:** lender switch under time pressure without clear confirmation of whether the issue was user error vs product withdrawal.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Revenue recognition/reporting delay if **David Gillen** report continues to slip.</p><p>- Repeatable quality risk if lender switches are made without documented checks and rationale.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>1. **Issue David Gillen’s outstanding report** and confirm logged/sent in CRM.</p><p>2. For lender switches (e.g., Barclays → Skipton), ensure CRM notes record **why**, **what was checked**, and **client confirmation** where relevant.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)** (with **Admin** support if report processing requires it)</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** output is holding, but report completion and documentation discipline needs tightening.</p><p>---</p><p>## Projected Income / Pipeline Health</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Quarter delivered: **£20k**.</p><p>- Quarter target: **£45k**.</p><p>- Remaining requirement: **£25k** to hit target.</p><p>- Performance expectation reference: written needs to be **£30k–£35k+** consistently to remain in bonus territory quarter-on-quarter (manager expectation).</p><p>- Craig reports many clients are viewing, but offer acceptance timing is uncertain.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior quarter-to-date trend provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Active pipeline exists; Craig reports engaged clients providing regular updates.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Conversion timing risk: many active viewers without accepted offers creates uncertainty and time load.</p><p>- Reliance on “fair wind” suggests progression discipline may not be consistent enough to smooth outcomes.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Delivery risk:** £25k remaining is a large gap dependent on offer acceptance and progression speed.</p><p>- **Capacity risk:** if multiple viewers convert simultaneously, admin and follow-up load may spike.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>1. Apply the **~1 month then hand to Sean** rule for low-engagement cases and ensure the **handover is recorded in CRM**.</p><p>2. Use structured chasers/tasks to move cases faster from **offer accepted → DIP/application**.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser (Craig)** / **Other (Sean)** for handovers</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant:** pipeline volume exists, but conversion is not yet predictable enough to close the quarter gap.</p><p>---</p><p>## Overall Performance Diagnosis</p><p>#### Adviser strengths this week</p><p>- Lead volume and reported quality remain strong; recent buyers progressing to document stage.</p><p>- Workflow discipline is improved versus prior behaviour described.</p><p>- Increasing self-awareness and earlier positioning of protection.</p><p>#### Main risks</p><p>- Protection shortfall is primarily **value (average premium)** and **fee consistency**, not only penetration.</p><p>- CRM/reporting integrity issues from **9 “created Sean”** leads, low note volume reference, and stale appointment outcomes.</p><p>- Month-end discipline gaps (e.g., **David Gillen** report outstanding).</p><p>- Diary allocation changes (Karen weekend/branch involvement) risk redistributing leads without uplift.</p><p>#### Behaviour patterns</p><p>- Mortgage process priority under pressure can push protection later unless deliberately diarised and recorded.</p><p>- Informal handover rules exist, but must be made explicit in CRM to prevent ownership gaps.</p><p>#### Where management support is required</p><p>- Confirm weekend/branch cover operating model and success measures for the Karen trial.</p><p>- Confirm Platform/workflow expectations and fix routing issues where errors are identified.</p><p>---</p><p>## Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---|---|---|</p><p>| Issue outstanding report and confirm logged/sent in CRM | **David Gillen** | Adviser (Craig) / Admin (if needed) | Not stated | Month-end output and revenue timing impacted |</p><p>| Review and correct the **9 leads marked “created Sean”** (ownership, duplicates, correct outcomes) | Leads (last month) | Other (Sean) + Adviser (Craig) | Not stated | Misreporting, ownership gaps, lost follow-ups |</p><p>| Update outcomes for the **4 “appointment booked”** leads if appointments have passed | Leads (last month) | Adviser (Craig) | Not stated | Pipeline drift; inaccurate forecasting |</p><p>| Resolve overdue workflow item and log next step/handover | **Lewis McCourt** | Adviser (Craig) | Not stated | Missed follow-up; reduced conversion chance |</p><p>| Approve uploaded documents in Platform so they auto-transfer into Midas | **Mark Beverstock** | Adviser (Craig) | Not stated | Case stalls due to documents not flowing into Midas |</p><p>| Investigate and resolve ID check “not quite matching” issue | **Mark Beverstock** | Adviser (Craig) | Not stated | ID verification failure may block application progress |</p><p>| Book and complete life insurance quote call | Protection (client name not stated) | Adviser (Craig) | **2026-04-02** | Client disengages or sources elsewhere; protection shortfall continues |</p><p>| Use Sean to nudge quiet/stalled leads/cases promptly | Stalled/quiet pipeline | Adviser (Craig) / Other (Sean) | **2026-04-05** | Drift increases slippage; written consistency risk |</p><p>| Arrange meeting to agree weekend/branch cover trial and success measures focused on uplift (not redistribution) | Lead generation / weekend cover model | Other (Bob) | **2026-04-08** | Lead dilution; no measurable uplift; diary instability |</p><p>| Confirm whether Platform nurture/searching workflow is enabled; agree rollout/training or interim process | CRM/process adoption | Other (Bob / Enablement) | **2026-04-08** | Unstructured nurture; inconsistent engagement; reporting gaps |</p><p>| For lender switches, record rationale/checks/client confirmation in CRM | Process control | Adviser (Craig) | Not stated | Repeatable errors; delays and compliance/reporting risk |</p><p>---</p><p>## Email Recap (Auto-Generated From Review)</p><p>**Subject:** Weekly Performance Review and Agreed Actions</p><p>Hi Craig,</p><p>Below is a summary of this week’s review and agreed actions.</p><p>### Performance Snapshot</p><p>- **Leads (last month):** **44** total  </p><p>  - **9** marked as “created Sean” (ownership/source review required)  </p><p>  - **4** still showing as “appointment booked” (outcome/status update needed)  </p><p>  - **No-shows:** ~**1 per week**, including **1 no-show on 2026-04-01**</p><p>- **Workflow:** **2 overdue**, including **Lewis McCourt** overdue by **7 days**</p><p>- **Written business (month):** **12.978**, with **1 report still to go out** (**David Gillen**)</p><p>- **Protection (YTD):** **30/≈80** insured cases (**38%**)  </p><p>  - Main drag: **average premium £1,200** vs group **~£2,000**  </p><p>  - **Fee charging rate:** **38%** vs group **55%**</p><p>- **Quarter position:** **£20k** delivered vs **£45k** target (**£25k** remaining)</p><p>### What is Working</p><p>- Lead volume is strong and recent appointments have been higher quality (last two buyers have bought and are moving into documents).</p><p>- Platform document upload engagement is positive on **Mark Beverstock**.</p><p>### Identified Risks</p><p>- CRM/reporting integrity risk from **9 “created Sean”** leads and stale appointment outcomes.</p><p>- Protection underperformance is mainly driven by **low average premium** and **fee consistency**.</p><p>- Month-end admin slippage with **David Gillen** report outstanding.</p><p>- Weekend/branch cover trial risk: diary opportunities could be redistributed without uplift.</p><p>### Cases Requiring Attention</p><p>- **David Gillen:** report still to be issued (Owner: Craig/Admin).  </p><p>- **Lewis McCourt:** workflow overdue by **7 days**—confirm status and update next steps/handover (Owner: Craig).  </p><p>- **Mark Beverstock (Atom Bank):** approve Platform documents, resolve ID mismatch, confirm lender direction (Owner: Craig).</p><p>### Revenue and Pipeline Risks</p><p>- Quarter remains **£25k** short of target; conversion timing depends on progressing viewers to offer accepted and then through DIP/application.</p><p>- Protection value gap (average premium) and fee consistency continue to depress total revenue.</p><p>### Focus for Next Week</p><p>- Tighten CRM accuracy: correct ownership on “created Sean” leads and update stale appointment outcomes.</p><p>- Eliminate overdue workflow items and ensure every case has a logged next step or a recorded handover.</p><p>- Bring protection forward: diarise and record protection outcomes at DIP/application stage; complete the protection quote call on **2026-04-02**.</p><p>- Progress offer-accepted cases by removing document/ID bottlenecks (approve Platform docs promptly).</p><p>### Agreed Actions</p><p>| Action | Owner | Deadline |</p><p>|---|---|---|</p><p>| Issue outstanding report for **David Gillen** and confirm CRM updated | Craig (and Admin if needed) | Not stated |</p><p>| Review the **9 “created Sean”** leads from last month and correct ownership/status | Sean + Craig | Not stated |</p><p>| Check/update the **4** leads still showing as “appointment booked” | Craig | Not stated |</p><p>| Resolve overdue workflow item for **Lewis McCourt** and update next steps in CRM | Craig | Not stated |</p><p>| Approve Platform documents + resolve ID mismatch + confirm lender direction | Craig (Mark Beverstock) | Not stated |</p><p>| Complete life insurance quote call (client name not captured) | Craig | **2026-04-02** |</p><p>| Use Sean to nudge quiet/stalled leads/cases promptly | Craig / Sean | **2026-04-05** |</p><p>| Arrange meeting to agree weekend/branch cover trial and success measures | Bob | **2026-04-08** |</p><p>| Confirm Platform nurture/searching enablement and agree training or interim workflow | Bob / Enablement | **2026-04-08** |</p><p>If circumstances change during the week, CRM must be updated immediately and blockers flagged early.</p><p>Bob</p><p>Full transcript</p><p>Bob Duncan: G